{"shortDescription":"Asia's diesel market activity stayed upbeat on Friday, with window deals brisk despite softening cash premiums, while timespreads and margins both logged weekly gains.Spot sales for September cargoes remained the market's focus this week, with a handful of refiners still clearing their regular sale…","astDescription":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"p","children":["Asia's diesel market activity stayed upbeat on Friday, with window deals brisk despite softening cash premiums, while timespreads and margins both logged weekly gains."]},{"type":"p","children":["Spot sales for September cargoes remained the market's focus this week, with a handful of refiners still clearing their regular sale requirements though the discussion levels were in a wide range."]},{"type":"p","children":["Some cargoes were done in discounts, while a few deals were closed at near parity levels on a free on board basis."]},{"type":"p","children":["Early week, some buying interest from the west, such as Mexico, were prevalent. Traders were also eyeing potential shipments on the east-west route, given the wider discounts week on week."]},{"type":"p","children":["The east-west spread was trading at discounts of around $170 per metric ton, narrowing for a second straight session, though they were at wider discounts week on week."]},{"type":"p","children":["Refining margins (GO10SGCKMc1) eased for the first session in three, though they were still firmer week on week at nearly $70 a barrel."]},{"type":"p","children":["Cash differentials (GO10-SIN-DIF) gave back some of previous session's gains and closed the trading session at around $3.7 a barrel, easing back to levels last seen in end-July."]},{"type":"p","children":["In jet fuel markets, offers for China barrels were available through the week given the continuous easing of export controls by Beijing. Some deals were done at discounts of $1-3 a barrel."]},{"type":"p","children":["The arbitrage gap between Asia and the U.S. 